From: OCCTSwift ecosystem <noreply@secondmouse.au>
Subject: [PATCH] Modeling Algorithms - IntCurve_IntConicConic brings an angle into a turn in one
 step, so a zero radius or an infinite parameter no longer loops

IntCurve_IntConicConic::Perform for a line against a circle, a line against an ellipse and two
circles brings the first parameter of the conic's domain into [0, 2PI) by taking a turn off, or
adding one, while it is out of range. Two kinds of value make that loop endless:
 - an infinite parameter, which a turn does not change;
 - a finite one so large that a turn is below its last digit.
The domain is widened by its tolerance over the conic's radius before the second reduction
(`FirstTolerance() / Circle.Radius()`, `DE.FirstTolerance() / E.MinorRadius()`), so a radius of 0
gives an infinity and a radius that is merely tiny gives a huge value. gp_Circ2d and gp_Elips2d
accept a radius of 0, and BRepTools::Read builds a Geom2d_Circle of radius 0 from a BREP that
says so, which BRepCheck_Wire::SelfIntersect then hands to this class: BRepCheck_Analyzer never
returns.

The reduction is now std::fmod, which terminates for every input. An infinite or NaN angle stays
one, the comparisons after it fail, and the function returns with whatever it found.

Measured with a direct probe of the three overloads (23 cases, each in its own process with an
alarm): 10 hang before (a circle of radius 0, 1e-20, 1e-300 and the smallest denormal; an ellipse
with a minor radius of 0 and 1e-300; an infinite first or last parameter of a circle domain; an
infinite first parameter of the second circle of a circle pair), 0 after, and the 13 that returned
keep their number of points. BRepCheck_Analyzer on the four damaged files that reproduced it
answers false in place of never returning.

Tracked as SecondMouseAU/OCCTSwift#3506.

---
diff --git a/src/ModelingAlgorithms/TKGeomAlgo/IntCurve/IntCurve_IntConicConic_1.cxx b/src/ModelingAlgorithms/TKGeomAlgo/IntCurve/IntCurve_IntConicConic_1.cxx
